title: The full adder
mode: draw
chapter: Arithmetic
check: behaves
parts: or chip:my_half_adder
needs: my_xor my_half_adder
keep: my_full_adder
---- intro
To add numbers of many bits, each column adds **three** bits: the two digits, and the **carry** coming in from the column to its right (as in long addition on paper). The part that does it is the **full adder**: inputs `a`, `b` and `cin` (carry in), outputs `s` (sum) and `cout` (carry out).

You can make it from two of your half adders: the first adds `a` and `b`, the second adds the carry coming in to that sum. If either of them carries, the full adder carries.
---- task
Build the full adder from two **my_half_adder** chips and one OR gate.
---- start
input a 0 0
input b 0 70
input cin 0 160
led s 520 40
led cout 520 160
---- answer
input a 0 0
input b 0 70
input cin 0 160
chip h1 my_half_adder 120 0
chip h2 my_half_adder 270 60
or o1 400 150
led s 520 40
led cout 520 160
a -> h1.a
b -> h1.b
h1.sum -> h2.a
cin -> h2.b
h2.sum -> s
h1.carry -> o1.0
h2.carry -> o1.1
o1 -> cout
---- reference
input a, b, cin
led s, cout
s = a ^ b ^ cin
cout = (a & b) | (a & cin) | (b & cin)
---- hint1
The first half adder gets `a` and `b`. The second gets the first one's `sum` and `cin`.
---- hint2
The second half adder's `sum` is `s`. Each half adder has a `carry`: OR them together for `cout`.
---- solution
Half adder 1 adds `a` and `b`; half adder 2 adds `cin` to that sum and gives `s`. An OR of the two carries is `cout`. 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, binary 11: `s` 1, `cout` 1.
---- done
Chain these and you can add numbers of any size: the `cout` of one column goes into the `cin` of the next. Every processor adds like this. Your full adder is kept as a chip, **my_full_adder**: next, it adds numbers of two bits.
